And there is something historically remarkable there even if we keep theology completely separate. Christianity became the world’s largest religion, although obviously that outcome wasn’t produced by Jesus’ teaching technique alone—apostles, institutions, states, culture, conquest, printing, missionary movements, and two thousand years of history all matter. But the transmission architecture in the source material is unusually potent.

Here are some of the mechanisms I think are worth quarrying:

Jesus-pattern	Pantheon abstraction
Parables	Encode an operator in a story that requires the listener to perform part of the inference
“Who do you say that I am?” / questions	Make the learner generate the representation instead of merely receiving it
Good Samaritan	Break inherited categories by forcing the moral invariant to survive an identity swap
First becomes last; servant becomes leader	Perspective inversion / hierarchy rotation
Love your enemy	Break an adversarial feedback loop instead of optimizing inside it
Render unto Caesar…	Jurisdictional partitioning: distinguish authorities instead of forcing one ontology across everything
Sabbath was made for man…	Recover purpose beneath the rule; specification serves invariant, not vice versa
Mustard seed / leaven	Small seed → distributed nonlinear propagation
Bread/wine, washing feet, baptism	Encode abstract meaning into embodied, repeatable ritual
Twelve / sending disciples outward	Small trusted cell → delegated propagation → distributed network
Withdrawal into solitude	Deliberate state reset before continued execution
Healing + teaching together	Demonstration gives doctrine credibility; capability accompanies language
Eating with outsiders	Move the network boundary rather than merely arguing about inclusion
Forgiveness	Stop historical debt from indefinitely controlling the next state
Cross / sacrifice	Costly commitment: the operator is demonstrated under maximal adverse conditions

And that last category is important.

